\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIstring\fR
Remove the grant scoped to this environment within \-\-project (optional; requires \-\-project)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Remove the grant scoped to this project (optional)
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fIstring\fR
Role name to remove (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIstring\fR
User email address (required)
.SS keyorix rbac remove\-role\-from\-group
Remove a role from a group
.PP
Remove a role assignment from a group.
.PP
With \-\-project, removes only the grant scoped to that project. With \-\-environment, removes only the grant scoped to that environment within the project (\-\-project must also be supplied).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rbac remove\-role\-from\-group [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rbac remove\-role\-from\-group \-\-group platform\-team \-\-role developer \-\-project payments
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIstring\fR
Remove the grant scoped to this environment within \-\-project (optional; requires \-\-project)
.TP
\fB\-\-group\fR \fIstring\fR
Group name or numeric ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Remove the grant scoped to this project (optional)
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fIstring\fR
Role name to remove (required)
.SS keyorix request
Manage project access requests
.PP
Request access to a project, list, withdraw, and review (approve/reject) requests (ADR\-024).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request [command]\fR
.SS keyorix request access
Request access to a project
.PP
Create a pending access request for the caller to a project, optionally suggesting a role (ADR\-024).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request access [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request access \-\-project payments \-\-role developer \-\-reason "on\-call this week"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name (or use KEYORIX_PROJECT)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Project ID \-\- use this instead of \-\-project when the caller holds no project grant yet (nothing to list, so a name cannot be resolved); the project\-scoped access\-request routes themselves do not require it
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Reason for the request (optional)
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fIstring\fR
Suggested project role (optional)
.SS keyorix request bulk\-approve
Approve multiple pending access requests at once
.PP
Approve several pending access requests in a single call (ADR\-024 extension). Provide a comma\-separated list of request IDs with \-\-ids.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request bulk\-approve [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request bulk\-approve \-\-ids 4,5,6
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-ids\fR \fIstring\fR
Comma\-separated list of access request IDs (required)
.SS keyorix request bulk\-reject
Reject multiple pending access requests at once
.PP
Reject several pending access requests in a single call (ADR\-024 extension). All rejected requests share the same \-\-reason.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request bulk\-reject [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request bulk\-reject \-\-ids 7,8 \-\-reason "no business need"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-ids\fR \fIstring\fR
Comma\-separated list of access request IDs (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Rejection reason shared across all requests (required)
.SS keyorix request list
List a project's access requests
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request list \-\-project payments
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name (or use KEYORIX_PROJECT)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Project ID \-\- use this instead of \-\-project when the caller holds no project grant yet (nothing to list, so a name cannot be resolved); the project\-scoped access\-request routes themselves do not require it
.SS keyorix request rejection\-templates
Manage rejection\-reason templates
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request rejection\-templates [command]\fR
.SS keyorix request rejection\-templates add
Add a new rejection\-reason template
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request rejection\-templates add [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request rejection\-templates add \-\-name no\-need \-\-reason "No business need for this access"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Short label for the template (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Template rejection text (required)
.SS keyorix request rejection\-templates delete
Delete a rejection\-reason template by ID
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request rejection\-templates delete <id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request rejection\-templates delete 2
.fi
.SS keyorix request rejection\-templates list
List all rejection\-reason templates
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request rejection\-templates list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request rejection\-templates list
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix request review
Approve or reject a pending access request
.PP
Resolve a pending access request (admin\-driven, ADR\-024). Approving grants the role at the project scope (\-\-role overrides the suggested role); rejecting records a \-\-reason.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request review [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request review \-\-project payments \-\-id 4 \-\-action approve \-\-ttl 8h
keyorix request review \-\-project payments \-\-id 5 \-\-action reject \-\-reason "ask your manager first"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-action\fR \fIstring\fR
approve | reject (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Access request ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name (or use \-\-project\-id / KEYORIX_PROJECT)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Project ID \-\- use this instead of \-\-project when the caller holds no project grant yet (nothing to list, so a name cannot be resolved); the project\-scoped access\-request routes themselves do not require it
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Reason on reject
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fIstring\fR
Role to grant on approve (defaults to the suggested role)
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fIstring\fR
Time\-bound the granted role on approve (Go duration, e.g. 4h); empty = permanent
.SS keyorix request secret\-access
Request approval to read one restricted secret's value
.PP
Create a pending, secret\-scoped access request: approval to read ONE specific secret's value, distinct from `request access`'s broader project/role request. Only relevant when classification.restricted_requires_approval is enabled and the target secret's classification is "restricted" \(em the gate this satisfies.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request secret\-access [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request secret\-access \-\-ref payments/production/STRIPE_KEY \-\-reason "debugging INC\-1234"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Reason for the request (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-ref\fR \fIstring\fR
Secret reference "project/environment/name" (or use \-\-secret\-id)
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Secret ID (or use \-\-ref)
.SS keyorix request withdraw
Withdraw your own pending access request
.PP
Cancel a pending access request you own, by ID (self\-service, ADR\-024).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix request withdraw [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix request withdraw \-\-project payments \-\-id 4
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Access request ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name (or use \-\-project\-id / KEYORIX_PROJECT)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Project ID \-\- use this instead of \-\-project when the caller holds no project grant yet (nothing to list, so a name cannot be resolved); the project\-scoped access\-request routes themselves do not require it
.SS keyorix risk
Risk register \(em governed, time\-bound exceptions to control gaps (A.5.8)
.PP
Record and review accepted risk exceptions: a known control gap, accepted by an owner with a justification, that sunsets at a fixed date. An active exception is the evidence that a gap is governed (accepted with a deadline) rather than ignored.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix risk [command]\fR
.SS keyorix risk add
Record a risk exception (accept a control gap for a bounded time)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix risk add [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix risk add \-\-title "Shared admin account" \-\-category mfa \-\-justification "vendor limitation" \-\-expires 2027\-01\-31T00:00:00Z
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-category\fR \fIstring\fR
sod | mfa | rotation | dormant_access | classification | other (default other)
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIstring\fR
When the exception sunsets (RFC3339, required)
.TP
\fB\-\-justification\fR \fIstring\fR
Why the risk is accepted (required, recorded for audit)
.TP
\fB\-\-reference\fR \fIstring\fR
What it applies to (a user, an SoD pair, a secret)
.TP
\fB\-\-title\fR \fIstring\fR
Short name for the accepted risk (required)
.SS keyorix risk approve
Approve a risk exception so it takes effect (dual control \(em must not be its own creator)
.PP
An exception is inert until approved: it does not suppress its matched violation from the compliance posture until a DIFFERENT system.write holder than the one who created it approves it. This prevents a single principal from unilaterally creating and self\-approving a suppression of their own risk.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix risk approve [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix risk approve \-\-id 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
ID of the exception to approve (required)
.SS keyorix risk list
List risk exceptions (active by default; \-\-all includes expired)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix risk list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix risk list \-\-all
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Include expired exceptions (history)
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix risk revoke
Revoke a risk exception before its expiry
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix risk revoke [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix risk revoke \-\-id 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
ID of the exception to revoke (required)
.SS keyorix rotation
Manage secret\-rotation policies and see rotation posture
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation [command]\fR
.SS keyorix rotation create
Create a rotation policy
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation create \-\-name prod\-90d \-\-scope project \-\-project\-id 3 \-\-interval\-days 90 \-\-alert\-days\-before 14
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-alert\-days\-before\fR \fIint\fR
Warn this many days before the deadline (0\-90) (default 7)
.TP
\fB\-\-description\fR \fIstring\fR
Policy description
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Target environment (required when \-\-scope environment)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\-days\fR \fIint\fR
Rotation interval in days, 1\-365 (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Policy name (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-notify\-on\-breach\fR
Notify when a covered secret goes overdue (default true)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Target project (required when \-\-scope project)
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fIstring\fR
Scope: project | environment (required)
.SS keyorix rotation delete
Delete a rotation policy
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation delete <id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation delete 2
.fi
.SS keyorix rotation list
List rotation policies
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation list \-\-project\-id 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Filter by environment
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Filter by project
.SS keyorix rotation order
Print a safe rotation sequence for a project's dependency graph
.PP
Print the order in which a project's secrets should be rotated so that each secret is rotated before anything that depends on it (a topological sort of the dependency graph, ADR\-052). Only secrets that appear in the graph are listed.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation order <project\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation order 3
.fi
.SS keyorix rotation plan
Print an automated rotation plan (dependency\-safe waves, by urgency)
.PP
Print the rotation plan for a project: its overdue and due\-soon secrets, batched into dependency\-safe waves (rotate a wave at a time, top to bottom; within a wave the most urgent first) and annotated with why each is due and what it must follow (ADR\-053).
.PP
With \-\-all\-projects, print the deployment\-wide roll\-up instead: every project's plan aggregated into install\-wide totals, most pressing project first. Dependencies are per\-project, so each project keeps its own wave order.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation plan [project\-id] [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation plan 3
keyorix rotation plan \-\-all\-projects
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-projects\fR
Plan rotation across every project (deployment\-wide roll\-up)
.SS keyorix rotation show
Show a rotation policy
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation show <id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation show 2
.fi
.SS keyorix rotation status
Show policy\-covered secrets that are overdue or approaching rotation
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix rotation status [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix rotation status \-\-project\-id 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Limit to a single project
.SS keyorix run
Inject secrets as env vars and run a command
.PP
Fetch secrets for a project + environment, expose them as environment variables, then execute the supplied command.
.PP
\&'keyorix run' does not inject anything by default \-\- pick exactly one:
.PP
.nf
  \-\-var NAME=secret\-ref   (recommended) YOU choose the env var name.
    keyorix run \-\-env production \-\-var DATABASE_URL=db\-password \-\- node app.js
    Repeatable. Only the named secrets are injected, under the names you gave
    them \-\- a secret's own NAME never determines what env var it lands under.
.fi
.PP
.nf
  \-\-derive\-names   (deprecated) inject every secret in the project + environment,
    with the env var key AUTO\-DERIVED from the secret's own name. Deprecated
    because whoever can NAME a secret then controls which env var it becomes \-\-
    including reserved ones like LD_PRELOAD \-\- not just its value. See
    https://github.com/keyorixhq/keyorix/issues/1816 for why this changed.
.fi
.PP
Project is resolved via: \-\-project\-id flag \-> \-\-project flag \-> KEYORIX_PROJECT env.
.PP
\-\-project\-id bypasses the project\-NAME lookup (GET /api/v1/projects). Since #2780 that listing serves a project\-scoped caller their own projects, so \-\-project works for a token scoped to this project. \-\-project\-id is still the only path for a token with NO project grant at all (it resolves nothing by name), and the cheaper one in CI \-\- it skips a round trip. See https://github.com/keyorixhq/keyorix/issues/2360 for the same shape on 'keyorix request'.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix run [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Run a command with secrets injected as environment variables
keyorix run \-\-project payments \-\-env production \-\-var DB_PASSWORD=payments/production/DB_PASSWORD \-\- ./server
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-clean\-env\fR
Start the child process with ONLY the injected secrets (plus a minimal PATH/HOME baseline) instead of the full inherited parent environment
.TP
\fB\-\-derive\-names\fR
Deprecated: inject every secret in the project+environment, deriving the env var name from the secret's own name
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIstring\fR
Environment name (e.g. production) (default development)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name (overrides KEYORIX_PROJECT)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\-id\fR \fIuint\fR
Project ID \-\- use this instead of \-\-project to skip the name lookup (GET /api/v1/projects) entirely; required when the caller's token holds no project grant, since a name cannot be resolved without a listing
.TP
\fB\-\-var\fR \fIstringArray\fR
Inject one secret under an explicit env var name: \-\-var NAME=secret\-ref (repeatable, recommended)
.SS keyorix secret
Manage secrets
.PP
Create, read, update, delete, and manage secrets and their metadata; bulk operations, rotation, export/import, and hygiene reports.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret access
List who can access a secret and at what effective level
.PP
Show the effective access list for a secret: every user who can read it, with their EFFECTIVE permission (for a project member, the higher of their role and any active share), the grant that gives it (SOURCE), and every grant they hold (GRANTS), so a share that elevates a role is visible. Expired shares and shares to users who are not project members grant nothing and are not listed. Global admins have implicit access and are not listed. Requires secrets.read.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret access [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret access \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret access\-log
Show a secret's recent reads (who/when/from where)
.PP
List the recent access\-log entries for a secret. Requires secrets.read.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret access\-log [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret access\-log \-\-id 42 \-\-days 7
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-days\fR \fIint\fR
Lookback in days (default server\-side: 30)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret acl
Manage per\-secret ACL grants (RBAC Phase 3)
.PP
Manage fine\-grained per\-secret access control (RBAC Phase 3).
.PP
An ACL grant allows a specific user to access one secret independently of their project role. Requires secrets.manage at the secret's project scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret acl [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret acl grant
Grant a user ACL access to a secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret acl grant [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret acl grant \-\-secret 42 \-\-user 12 \-\-perm secrets.read
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-perm\fR \fIstringArray\fR
Permission to grant (secrets.read or secrets.write); may be repeated
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID to grant access to
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIint\fR
User ID to grant access to
.SS keyorix secret acl list
List ACL grants for a secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret acl list <secret\-id> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret acl list 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix secret acl revoke
Revoke an ACL grant from a secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret acl revoke [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret acl revoke \-\-secret 42 \-\-acl 5
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-acl\fR \fIint\fR
ACL entry ID to revoke (from `acl list`)
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID to revoke the ACL from
.SS keyorix secret audit
Show a secret's lifecycle events (created/rotated/suspended/shared/...)
.PP
List the audit trail for a secret: what happened to it and when (created, rotated, rolled\-back, suspended/resumed, shared, owner\-transferred, reclassified), newest first. Requires secrets.read at the secret's scope. Never prints a plaintext value.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret audit [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret audit \-\-id 42 \-\-limit 20
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIint\fR
Max events to show (default server\-side: 50, cap 500)
.SS keyorix secret auto\-rotate
Enable/disable automated rotation for a secret
.PP
Opt a secret into automated rotation: when it is overdue under an active rotation policy, Keyorix regenerates its value on schedule. Optionally set the generated value's shape with \-\-length and \-\-charset. Use \-\-off to disable.
.PP
Enable only for secrets whose value Keyorix owns \(em auto\-rotation changes the stored value without touching any upstream system. Requires secrets.write at the secret's scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret auto\-rotate [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Rotate secret 42 automatically with a generated 32\-character value
keyorix secret auto\-rotate \-\-id 42 \-\-length 32
keyorix secret auto\-rotate \-\-id 42 \-\-off
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-backend\fR \fIstring\fR
Rotation backend name \(em rotate the upstream credential too
.TP
\fB\-\-charset\fR \fIstring\fR
alphanumeric|lower_alphanumeric|hex|alphanumeric_symbols (empty = default)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-length\fR \fIint\fR
Generated value length (8\-256, 0 = default 32)
.TP
\fB\-\-off\fR
Disable auto\-rotation (default enables)
.TP
\fB\-\-ref\fR \fIstring\fR
Upstream identifier the backend rotates (required with \-\-backend)
.SS keyorix secret blast\-radius
Show the enriched blast radius of rotating or deleting a secret
.PP
Show all downstream dependents of a secret (recursive), each annotated with owner, project, hop depth, and risk level (critical/high/medium/low).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret blast\-radius <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret blast\-radius 42
.fi
.SS keyorix secret bulk\-delete
Delete multiple secrets in one operation
.PP
Delete one or more secrets in a project in a single call.
.PP
Pass secret IDs directly with \-\-ids, or resolve by name with \-\-names (requires \-\-project and \-\-env \(em a secret name is only unique within one project's environment, not across the whole project). \-\-confirm is mandatory for the deletion to proceed; without it the command prints what would be deleted and exits.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret bulk\-delete [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Preview, then confirm
keyorix secret bulk\-delete \-\-project 3 \-\-env 2 \-\-names OLD_KEY,UNUSED_TOKEN
keyorix secret bulk\-delete \-\-project 3 \-\-env 2 \-\-names OLD_KEY,UNUSED_TOKEN \-\-confirm
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-confirm\fR
Required to actually delete (omit to preview)
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required for \-\-names)
.TP
\fB\-\-ids\fR \fIintSlice\fR
Comma\-separated secret IDs to delete
.TP
\fB\-\-names\fR \fIstringSlice\fR
Comma\-separated secret names to delete (requires \-\-project and \-\-env)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required for \-\-names and for cross\-project guard)
.SS keyorix secret bulk\-rename
Rename secrets toward naming\-policy conformance
.PP
Rename one or more secrets in a project in a single call \(em the remediation for the secrets that "secret name\-conformance" lists as violating the current naming policy.
.PP
Each new name must itself satisfy the policy (you can only rename TOWARD conformance) and must not collide with another secret in the same environment; any entry failing a check is skipped with a reason while the rest proceed.
.PP
Specify renames as \-\-rename <id>=<new\-name> (repeatable). By default this is a DRY RUN that only reports what would change; pass \-\-apply to actually rename. Requires secrets.write at the project scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret bulk\-rename [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Dry run; add \-\-apply to rename
keyorix secret bulk\-rename \-\-project 3 \-\-rename 42=DB_PASSWORD \-\-rename 43=DB_USER
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
Actually rename (default is a dry run)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-rename\fR \fIstringArray\fR
Rename as <id>=<new\-name> (repeatable)
.SS keyorix secret bulk\-rotate
Trigger rotation for multiple secrets at once
.PP
Trigger rotation for all matching secrets in a project \(em the CLI surface for incident response ("rotate everything tagged confidential in project X").
.PP
Secrets without auto\-rotate configured are skipped (reported in the failure list, not treated as errors) so the operation is always best\-effort.
.PP
Without \-\-names the command rotates every matching secret in the project; use \-\-env and/or \-\-classification to narrow the scope.
.PP
\-\-confirm is required: this is a bulk destructive operation and an accidental wide rotation cannot be undone.
.PP
\-\-names requires \-\-env: a secret name is only unique within one project's environment, not across the whole project.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret bulk\-rotate [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret bulk\-rotate \-\-project 3 \-\-classification confidential \-\-confirm
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-classification\fR \fIstring\fR
Classification label filter (e.g. confidential)
.TP
\fB\-\-confirm\fR
Required: confirm you want to trigger bulk rotation
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (optional filter)
.TP
\fB\-\-names\fR \fIstring\fR
Comma\-separated secret names to rotate (omit to rotate all matching)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret cert
Show a certificate secret's public X.509 metadata (expiry, issuer, SANs)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret cert <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret cert 42
.fi
.SS keyorix secret classify
Set a secret's data\-classification label (ISO 27001 A.5.12)
.PP
Label a secret with its data\-sensitivity classification: public, internal, confidential, or restricted (or empty to clear). Requires secrets.write.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret classify [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret classify \-\-id 42 \-\-level confidential
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-level\fR \fIstring\fR
public|internal|confidential|restricted (empty clears)
.SS keyorix secret clear\-schedule
Remove the temporal access schedule for a secret (idempotent)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret clear\-schedule <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret clear\-schedule 42
.fi
.SS keyorix secret comment
Manage secret version comments
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret comment [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret comment add
Add a comment to a secret version
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret comment add <secret\-id> <version\-id> <comment>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret comment add 42 3 "rotated after the 2026\-10 incident"
.fi
.SS keyorix secret comment delete
Delete a comment from a secret version
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret comment delete <secret\-id> <version\-id> <comment\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret comment delete 42 3 7
.fi
.SS keyorix secret comment list
List comments on a secret version
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret comment list <secret\-id> <version\-id> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret comment list 42 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix secret copy
Copy a secret's value and metadata into another environment
.PP
Promote a secret into another environment of the same project (e.g. staging \-> production) without re\-typing the value. Creates a new, independently\-owned secret with the source's value, type, classification, and description.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret copy [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret copy \-\-id 42 \-\-to\-environment 2 \-\-name DB_PASSWORD_COPY
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Source secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Name for the copy (default: the source name)
.TP
\fB\-\-to\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Target environment ID, same project (required)
.SS keyorix secret copy\-environment
Copy every secret from one environment into another (same project)
.PP
Bulk\-promote a whole environment \-\- copy every secret from one environment into another of the same project (e.g. seed production from staging) in a single call. Name clashes in the target are skipped, never overwritten.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret copy\-environment [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret copy\-environment \-\-project 3 \-\-from\-environment 1 \-\-to\-environment 2
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Source environment ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-to\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Target environment ID, same project (required)
.SS keyorix secret create
Create a new secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Prompts for the value on a hidden line
keyorix secret create \-\-project 3 \-\-environment 2 \-\-name DB_PASSWORD \-\-interactive
# Or read it from a file
keyorix secret create \-\-project 3 \-\-environment 2 \-\-name TLS_KEY \-\-from\-file ./tls.key
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-description\fR \fIstring\fR
Optional free\-text note about the secret
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID. With \-\-project and no \-\-environment the project's "development" environment is used; if it has none, \-\-environment is required (see `keyorix project environments <project\-id>`). Without \-\-project: 1 (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIstring\fR
Expiration time (RFC3339 format)
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-file\fR \fIstring\fR
Read the secret value from a file (relative path), or from stdin with "\-"
.TP
\fB\-\-interactive\fR
Interactive mode (value read from a hidden prompt)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-reads\fR \fIint\fR
Maximum number of reads (0 = unlimited)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Secret name (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-type\fR \fIstring\fR
Secret type (default generic)
.SS keyorix secret delete
Delete a secret (soft\-delete; restorable with 'secret restore')
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret delete [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret delete \-\-id 42
keyorix secret delete \-\-project 3 \-\-environment 2 \-\-name DB_PASSWORD \-\-force
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required with \-\-name) (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Skip confirmation prompt, and delete even if other secrets depend on this one
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: text or json (json is non\-interactive and requires \-\-force) (default text)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Secret name
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required with \-\-name) (default 1)
.SS keyorix secret deps
Inspect and manage a secret's dependency graph
.PP
Manage the secret dependency graph (ADR\-052): declare that one secret depends on another, list a secret's dependencies and dependents, and see the blast radius of rotating it.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret deps [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret deps add
Declare that <secret\-id> depends on <depends\-on\-id>
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret deps add <secret\-id> <depends\-on\-id> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Secret 42 depends on secret 40
keyorix secret deps add 42 40 \-\-note "uses the same database"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fIstring\fR
Optional note describing the dependency
.SS keyorix secret deps impact
Show the blast radius of rotating a secret (transitive dependents)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret deps impact <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret deps impact 42
.fi
.SS keyorix secret deps list
Show what a secret depends on and what depends on it
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret deps list <secret\-id> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret deps list 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix secret deps rm
Remove a dependency edge (edge id from `deps list`)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret deps rm <secret\-id> <edge\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret deps rm 42 6
.fi
.SS keyorix secret description
Show or set a secret's description
.PP
Show a secret's free\-text note, or replace it with \-\-set.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
  keyorix secret description \-\-id 42                       # show the note
  keyorix secret description \-\-id 42 \-\-set "prod DB; dba@" # set the note
  keyorix secret description \-\-id 42 \-\-set ""              # clear it
.fi
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret description [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret description \-\-id 42 \-\-set "Primary orders database password"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-set\fR \fIstring\fR
Replace the description with this text ("" clears)
.SS keyorix secret diff
Diff metadata between two versions of a secret
.PP
Compare metadata between two versions of a secret.
.PP
Values (encrypted ciphertext) are never compared \-\- only classification, expiry, read count, and the current ACL snapshot.
.PP
<name> is resolved to a secret ID via \-\-project/\-\-environment (both required, matching every other by\-name lookup in this command group \-\- GET /api/v1/secrets/by\-name needs a numeric project_id/environment_id, not a name filter), unless \-\-id is given directly.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret diff <name> <from\-version> <to\-version> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret diff DB_PASSWORD 2 3 \-\-project 3 \-\-environment 2
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required unless \-\-id is set)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (skips the <name> lookup below when set)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required unless \-\-id is set)
.SS keyorix secret expiring
List a project's secrets expiring (or already expired) within a window
.PP
Show a project's secrets expiring soon (or already expired), soonest\-first, so you can renew or rotate them before they lapse. Requires secrets.read at the project scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret expiring [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret expiring \-\-project 3 \-\-days 14
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-days\fR \fIint\fR
Window in days (default server\-side: 30, cap 3650)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret explain
Explain the risk of a discovered secret and how to fix it
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret explain <key\-name>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret explain AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
.fi
.SS keyorix secret export
Export secrets to a file or stdout
.PP
Export secrets from Keyorix to dotenv, JSON, Vault YAML, or encrypted\-json format.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
  keyorix secret export \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3 \-\-format dotenv
  keyorix secret export \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3 \-\-format json \-\-output secrets.json
  keyorix secret export \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3 \-\-format vault \-\-output vault\-export.yaml
  keyorix secret export \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3 \-\-encrypt\-for recipient.pub.pem \-\-output secrets.enc.json
.fi
.PP
Supported formats:
.nf
  dotenv         .env files (KEY=VALUE)
  json           Flat key\-value JSON object
  vault          Medusa/Vault YAML (importable back via 'keyorix secret import \-\-format vault')
  encrypted\-json RSA\-OAEP\-SHA256 + AES\-256\-GCM encrypted envelope (safe for airgap transfer)
.fi
.PP
Output goes to stdout unless \-\-output is specified. Warnings and summary are always printed to stderr.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret export [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret export \-\-project 3 \-\-env 2 \-\-format dotenv \-\-output .env
keyorix secret export \-\-project 3 \-\-env 2 \-\-format json
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-encrypt\-for\fR \fIstring\fR
Path to RSA public key PEM (switches format to encrypted\-json)
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: dotenv, json, vault, encrypted\-json (default dotenv)
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output file path (default: stdout)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret fix
Fix a hardcoded secret by moving it to an environment variable
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret fix <key\-name> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Preview what would be moved out of source into .env
keyorix secret fix AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY \-\-path ./src
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Preview changes without applying (default: true) (default true)
.TP
\fB\-\-env\-file\fR \fIstring\fR
Target .env file for extracted secrets (default \&.env)
.TP
\fB\-\-interactive\fR
Step through each fix interactively
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fIstring\fR
Path to scan for the secret (default \&.)
.SS keyorix secret folder
Manage secret folders
.PP
Commands for creating, listing, and deleting secret folder nodes.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret folder [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret folder create
Create a folder
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret folder create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret folder create \-\-project 3 \-\-environment 2 \-\-name databases
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Folder name (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIint\fR
Parent folder ID (0 = root)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (default 1)
.SS keyorix secret folder delete
Delete a folder
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret folder delete [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret folder delete \-\-id 5
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Skip confirmation prompt
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Folder ID to delete (required)
.SS keyorix secret folder list
List folders
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret folder list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret folder list \-\-project 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.TP
\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIint\fR
Filter by parent folder ID (0 = no filter)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Filter by project ID
.SS keyorix secret get
Get a secret
.PP
Retrieve a secret by ID, by name, or by a project/environment/name reference.
.PP
\-\-ref reads the secret's value by a human\-readable "project/environment/name" reference (ADR\-059) \-\- handy for scripts and automation. It always reads the value (so it counts toward max_reads and is audited like any value read).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret get [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret get \-\-id 42
# Read the value by reference
keyorix secret get \-\-ref payments/production/DB_PASSWORD
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required with \-\-name) (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Secret name
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required with \-\-name) (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-ref\fR \fIstring\fR
Reference: project/environment/name (reads the value)
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-value\fR
Show the decrypted secret value
.SS keyorix secret get\-schedule
Show the temporal access schedule for a secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret get\-schedule <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret get\-schedule 42
.fi
.SS keyorix secret import
Import secrets from a file
.PP
Import secrets from a local file.
.PP
.nf
  keyorix secret import \-\-file .env \-\-format dotenv \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3
  keyorix secret import \-\-file vault\-export.yaml \-\-format vault \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3
  keyorix secret import \-\-file secrets.json \-\-format json \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3
  keyorix secret import \-\-file .env \-\-format dotenv \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3 \-\-dry\-run
  keyorix secret import \-\-file secrets.enc.json \-\-decrypt\-with private.pem \-\-project 7 \-\-env 3
.fi
.PP
Supported file formats (\-\-format):
.nf
  dotenv  .env files (KEY=VALUE, comments and blank lines ignored)
  vault   Medusa/Vault YAML export (path hierarchy, last two segments become name)
  json    Flat key\-value JSON object
.fi
.PP
Encrypted imports (\-\-decrypt\-with):
.nf
  When the file is a keyorix\-encrypted\-export\-v1 envelope (produced by
\&  'keyorix secret export \-\-format encrypted\-json'), pass the RSA private key
  with \-\-decrypt\-with. The file is decrypted transparently and then imported
  as a standard JSON payload.
.fi
.PP
Importing from a live source (Vault/AWS/Azure/GCP) is not supported by this command \(em use the old CLI's 'secret import \-\-source' until the dedicated migration tool exists.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret import [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret import \-\-project 3 \-\-env 2 \-\-file .env \-\-format dotenv \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-decrypt\-with\fR \fIstring\fR
Path to RSA private key PEM to decrypt an encrypted\-json export
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be imported without creating anything
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIint\fR
Environment ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fIstring\fR
Path to the file to import (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIstring\fR
File format: dotenv, vault, json (default dotenv)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-skip\-existing\fR
Skip secrets that already exist instead of failing (default true)
.SS keyorix secret info
Show a secret's metadata (no value)
.PP
Print a one\-shot summary of a secret's metadata \-\- type, classification, status, description, owner, expiration, and tags \-\- without retrieving the value. Requires secrets.read at the secret's scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret info [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret info \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret list
List secrets
.PP
List secrets with filtering and pagination.
.PP
The server applies authentication\-based filtering automatically: you see only what you're authorized to read.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret list \-\-project payments
keyorix secret list \-\-project payments \-o json
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-environment\fR \fIint\fR
Filter by environment ID (0 = all)
.TP
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIint\fR
Maximum number of results (default 50)
.TP
\fB\-\-offset\fR \fIint\fR
Number of results to skip
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIstring\fR
Project name or numeric ID
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fIstring\fR
Search query
.SS keyorix secret move
Move a secret or folder to a different parent folder
.PP
Move a secret or folder to a different parent folder.
.PP
Use \-\-to 0 (or omit \-\-to) to move the node to the root (no parent).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret move [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret move \-\-id 42 \-\-to 5
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret or folder ID to move (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fIint\fR
Target parent folder ID (0 = root)
.SS keyorix secret name\-conformance
List secrets whose names violate the current naming policy
.PP
Show the live secrets whose names fail the current secret naming policy. The policy is enforced only when a secret is created, so names can fall out of conformance after the policy is added or tightened \(em this surfaces those stragglers so you can rename them.
.PP
With \-\-project, scopes to one project (requires secrets.read at the project scope). Without \-\-project, reports across every project (requires audit.read \(em admin view).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret name\-conformance [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret name\-conformance \-\-project 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (omit for an org\-wide admin view)
.SS keyorix secret orphaned
List secrets whose owner is no longer a live user
.PP
Show a project's secrets whose owner has been deleted (offboarding hygiene). Each is left without an accountable owner \(em re\-assign one with 'keyorix secret reassign\-owner'. Requires secrets.read at the project scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret orphaned [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret orphaned \-\-project 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret ownership\-history
Show a secret's ownership\-transfer chain
.PP
List all ownership transfers for a secret in chronological order. Each record shows who the secret was transferred from, who it was transferred to, who performed the transfer, and when. Requires secrets.read at the secret's scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret ownership\-history [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret ownership\-history \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret quota\-report
Show secrets approaching or at their MaxReads quota
.PP
List all secrets with a MaxReads cap configured, showing current read count, quota percentage, and status (ok / warning / critical / exhausted). Requires audit.read (a usage\-report endpoint, not secret\-value access).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret quota\-report\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret quota\-report
.fi
.SS keyorix secret reassign\-owner
Re\-home all of a departed user's secrets to a new owner
.PP
Transfer every secret in a project owned by one user to another, in one call \(em the bulk completion of offboarding after 'keyorix secret orphaned' surfaces a gone owner's secrets. Requires roles.assign at the project scope (the same blast radius as a role grant, not secrets.write); each secret is authorized individually.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret reassign\-owner [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret reassign\-owner \-\-project 3 \-\-from 12 \-\-to 15
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIint\fR
Current owner's user ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fIint\fR
New owner's user ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret render
Render a template, expanding ${secret:<environment>/<name>} references
.PP
Render a template file (or stdin) to stdout (or \-\-output), replacing each ${secret:<environment>/<name>} placeholder with that secret's current value.
.PP
References are resolved within a single project (\-\-project, a numeric ID). Only secrets you can read there are resolved; a missing or forbidden reference fails the render without writing partial output.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
  keyorix secret render app.env.tpl \-o app.env \-\-project 7
  cat app.env.tpl | keyorix secret render \-\-project 7
.fi
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret render [template\-file] [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret render config.tmpl \-\-project 3 \-\-output config.yaml
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Write to this file instead of stdout
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret restore
Restore a soft\-deleted secret
.PP
Restore a soft\-deleted secret by clearing its deletion, returning it to the live list. Find restorable secrets with 'secret trash \-\-project <id>'.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret restore [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret restore \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret resume
Resume a suspended secret \-\- restore value reads
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret resume [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret resume \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret rollback
Restore a secret to the value of a prior version
.PP
Restore a secret to an earlier version's value. The old value is re\-instated as a NEW version (history stays append\-only, so the rollback itself can be undone).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret rollback [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret rollback \-\-id 42 \-\-version 2
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR \fIint\fR
Version number to restore (required)
.SS keyorix secret rotate
Rotate a secret by providing a new value
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret rotate [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Prompts for the new value
keyorix secret rotate \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret rotation\-simulate
Simulate a rotation dry\-run for a secret
.PP
Validate a secret's rotation configuration without executing any live rotation.
.PP
Checks:
.nf
  policy_exists  \(em at least one active rotation policy covers the secret
  backend_known  \(em the configured rotation backend is registered
  ref_non_empty  \(em the rotation ref is non\-empty
  ref_valid      \(em the rotation ref passes metacharacter validation
.fi
.PP
Requires secrets.read at the secret's scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret rotation\-simulate [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret rotation\-simulate \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret scan
Scan a directory for secrets and hardcoded credentials
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret scan [path] [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret scan ./src
keyorix secret scan \-\-staged \-\-severity high
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-commit\fR \fIstring\fR
Scan files changed in a specific commit (e.g. HEAD~1)
.TP
\fB\-\-import\fR
Print a follow\-up 'secret import' hint after scanning (does not import automatically)
.TP
\fB\-\-report\fR \fIstring\fR
Save scan report to file (JSON format)
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIstring\fR
Filter by severity: low, medium, high
.TP
\fB\-\-staged\fR
Scan only git staged files
.SS keyorix secret score
Show the risk score for a secret
.PP
Print the composite risk score for a secret.
.PP
The score is 0\-100 (higher = riskier) and is broken down into four weighted factors: rotation age (30%), expiry (30%), usage (20%), and exposure (20%).
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret score [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret score \-\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret set\-schedule
Set a temporal access schedule for a secret
.PP
Restrict read access to a secret to a specific day\-of\-week + hour window.
.PP
The schedule applies to all permission\-checked reads (human users); admin/ machine reads bypass it.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret set\-schedule <secret\-id> [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Allow reads on weekdays, 08:00\-18:00 UTC
keyorix secret set\-schedule 42 \-\-days mon,tue,wed,thu,fri \-\-start\-hour 8 \-\-end\-hour 18
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-days\fR \fIstring\fR
Allowed days: comma\-separated day names (mon,tue,...) or ISO numbers (1\-7), or "*" for all days (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-end\-hour\fR \fIint\fR
End hour of the read window (1\-24, exclusive) (default 24)
.TP
\fB\-\-start\-hour\fR \fIint\fR
Start hour of the read window (0\-23, inclusive)
.TP
\fB\-\-timezone\fR \fIstring\fR
IANA timezone name (default: UTC) (default UTC)
.SS keyorix secret suspend
Suspend a secret \-\- block value reads without deleting it
.PP
Freeze a secret: value reads are refused until it is resumed. Versions, shares, and the audit trail are preserved. Requires secrets.write.
.PP
With \-\-clear\-synced\-copies the copies of this secret synced to Kubernetes clusters (by the operator or keyorix\-k8s\-sync) are also emptied for the duration of THIS suspension, whatever the resource's own onSuspend setting says. Use it when the secret is compromised, and rotate it afterwards. Resuming removes the request and the next sync restores the copies. Running suspend again with the flag on a secret that is already suspended adds the request.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret suspend [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret suspend \-\-id 42 \-\-reason "suspected leak"
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\-synced\-copies\fR
Also clear the copies synced to Kubernetes clusters for this suspension (compromise response; rotate the secret afterwards)
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Optional reason recorded in the audit event
.SS keyorix secret tags
List or set a secret's tags
.PP
Show a secret's tags, or replace them with \-\-set.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
  keyorix secret tags \-\-id 42                   # list the secret's tags
  keyorix secret tags \-\-id 42 \-\-set prod,tier1  # replace the tag set
  keyorix secret tags \-\-id 42 \-\-set ""          # clear all tags
.fi
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret tags [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret tags \-\-id 42 \-\-set db,prod
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-set\fR \fIstring\fR
Replace the secret's tags with this comma\-separated list ("" clears)
.SS keyorix secret template
Manage secret templates
.PP
Commands for creating, listing, getting and deleting reusable secret templates.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret template [command]\fR
.SS keyorix secret template create
Create a secret template
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret template create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret template create \-\-name db\-credential \-\-classification confidential \-\-tags db \-\-rotation\-hint\-days 90
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-classification\fR \fIstring\fR
Default classification (public|internal|confidential|restricted)
.TP
\fB\-\-description\fR \fIstring\fR
Template description
.TP
\fB\-\-description\-pattern\fR \fIstring\fR
Hint text for the description field
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIstring\fR
Template name (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-rotation\-hint\-days\fR \fIint\fR
Suggested rotation interval in days (informational)
.TP
\fB\-\-tags\fR \fIstring\fR
Default tags (comma\-separated, e.g. db,prod)
.SS keyorix secret template delete
Delete a secret template by name
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret template delete <name>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret template delete db\-credential
.fi
.SS keyorix secret template get
Get a secret template by name
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret template get <name>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret template get db\-credential
.fi
.SS keyorix secret template list
List all secret templates
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret template list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret template list
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix secret trash
List a project's soft\-deleted (restorable) secrets
.PP
Show the recycle bin for a project: secrets that were deleted but are still restorable (until the purge job reaps them), newest\-deleted first. Use the ID with 'secret restore \-\-id <id>'. Requires secrets.read at the project scope.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret trash [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret trash \-\-project 3
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIint\fR
Max entries to show (default server\-side: 100, cap 500)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIint\fR
Project ID (required)
.SS keyorix secret update
Update an existing secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret update [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
# Prompts for the new value
keyorix secret update \-\-id 42 \-\-interactive
keyorix secret update \-\-id 42 \-\-expires 2027\-01\-31T00:00:00Z
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\-expiration\fR
Remove expiration
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\-max\-reads\fR
Remove the read limit (unlimited)
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIstring\fR
Update expiration (RFC3339 format)
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-file\fR \fIstring\fR
Read the new value from a file (relative path), or from stdin with "\-"
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-interactive\fR
Interactive mode (value read from a hidden prompt)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-reads\fR \fIint\fR
Update max reads (\-1 = no change, minimum 1; use \-\-clear\-max\-reads to remove the limit) (default \-1)
.SS keyorix secret versions
List secret versions
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix secret versions [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix secret versions \-\-id 42 \-o json
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.SS keyorix share
Share secrets with users and groups
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share [command]\fR
.SS keyorix share create
Share a secret with another user or group
.PP
Share a secret with another user or group. The recipient must be a member of the secret's project.
.PP
.nf
  read   the recipient can read the secret.
  write  the recipient can also update its value and metadata (description, tags)
         and rotate it. A write share never lets them suspend or resume it, change
         its expiry or read limit, move it, roll it back, transfer ownership,
         re\-share it, change access rules or delete it: those need a project role.
.fi
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share create \-\-secret\-id 42 \-\-recipient\-id 12 \-\-permission read \-\-ttl 24h
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIstring\fR
Make the share time\-bound: absolute expiry (RFC3339, e.g. 2026\-07\-01T15:00:00Z)
.TP
\fB\-\-is\-group\fR
Whether the recipient is a group
.TP
\fB\-\-permission\fR \fIstring\fR
Permission level: read, or write (read + update value/metadata + rotate; see \-\-help) (default read)
.TP
\fB\-\-recipient\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Recipient ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fIstring\fR
Make the share time\-bound: lifetime from now (Go duration, e.g. 24h, 30m); mutually exclusive with \-\-expires
.SS keyorix share group\-shares
List shares for a group
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share group\-shares [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share group\-shares \-\-group\-id 4
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-group\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Group ID (required)
.SS keyorix share list
List shares for a secret
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share list [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share list \-\-secret\-id 42
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIstring\fR
Output format: table, json or yaml (default table)
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Secret ID (required)
.SS keyorix share revoke
Revoke a share
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share revoke [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share revoke \-\-share\-id 9
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-share\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Share ID (required)
.SS keyorix share self\-remove
Remove yourself from a secret shared with you
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share self\-remove <secret\-id>\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share self\-remove 42
.fi
.SS keyorix share shared\-secrets
List secrets shared with a user (defaults to yourself)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share shared\-secrets [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share shared\-secrets
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-user\-id\fR \fIint\fR
User ID (defaults to yourself; viewing another user's shared secrets requires an admin permission and rank over that user)
.SS keyorix share update
Update a share's permission
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix share update [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
keyorix share update \-\-share\-id 9 \-\-permission write \-\-ttl 48h
.fi
.PP
Flags:
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\-expiry\fR
Make the share permanent (remove its expiry)
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIstring\fR
Set/extend the time\-bound expiry: absolute (RFC3339)
.TP
\fB\-\-permission\fR \fIstring\fR
Permission level: read, or write (read + update value/metadata + rotate) (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-share\-id\fR \fIint\fR
Share ID (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fIstring\fR
Set/extend the time\-bound expiry: lifetime from now (Go duration, e.g. 24h); mutually exclusive with \-\-expires
.SS keyorix sod
Separation\-of\-duties policies and violations (ISO 27001 A.5.3)
.PP
Define toxic permission combinations and find the principals who hold both sides.
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix sod [command]\fR
.SS keyorix sod policy
Manage separation\-of\-duties policies
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix sod policy [command]\fR
.SS keyorix sod policy create
Create a separation\-of\-duties policy (two conflicting permissions)
.PP
Usage: \fBkeyorix sod policy create [flags]\fR
.PP
Examples:
